id: recipe.incident-diagnosis
kind: recipe
title: Kubernetes incident diagnosis
aliases: kubernetes incident, cluster incident, production down kubernetes
question: a Kubernetes workload broke in production -- how do you diagnose it?
tags: operations, kubernetes, incident
properties:
  - blast radius established early
  - state read before any restart
  - the timeline reconstructed from events
  - recovery and rollback paths identified before acting
invariant: Establish scope, read the declared-versus-observed state, reconstruct the timeline, and only then act -- restarts destroy evidence.
answer_30s: Scope the impact, read object status and events before touching anything, follow the layer that is broken (scheduling, image, probe, config, network), and choose rollback or roll-forward deliberately.
flow: |
  scope: what is broken, for whom, since when
     |
  cluster: nodes ready, control plane healthy, capacity available
     |
  workload: replicas desired vs ready, restarts, conditions
     |
  pod: events, container state (OOMKilled/ImagePullBackOff), probe results, mounts
     |
  network: endpoints, DNS, ingress errors
     |
  action: rollback (template) or fix-forward; record the timeline
compose:
  - k8s.debugging-workflow => the layer-ordered procedure
  - k8s.events => the timeline, including objects nobody thought to check
  - k8s.describe => spec, status, conditions and event tail in one page
  - k8s.logs => containers that started, and previous instances that crashed
  - k8s.endpoints => whether traffic is reaching anything
  - k8s.node => node conditions, pressure and evictions
  - k8s.etcd => when the failure is control-plane wide
  - k8s.scheduler => Pending pods and the predicate that failed
  - k8s.rollback => the fast, rehearsed recovery path
  - design.observability => which metric or alert told you first
  - design.health-checking => probes as both cause and symptom
failure_modes:
  - deleting pods before reading their state (destroying the evidence)
  - restarting the whole fleet because one replica was unhealthy
  - ignoring the empty endpoint list while debugging the application
  - rolling back code without checking that the schema change permits it
follow_ups:
  - how do you find the cause if the pods have already been replaced?
related:
  - k8s.debugging-workflow
  - k8s.events
  - k8s.rollback
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/
